faq:
  - q: "How is this different from a one-day AI workshop?"
    a: "A workshop teaches awareness; three months changes behaviour. Site staff sit through a workshop and are back to writing variations up on Sunday within a fortnight, because nobody built the habit on live jobs. Our program runs across three months on your own work, and the ongoing support keeps it current as the tools move."
  - q: "Will site staff actually use it?"
    a: "It starts from how they already work — voice notes and phones rather than desktop tools — which is what makes the difference. Supervisors and project managers pick it up fastest, and that's where the paperwork sits anyway."
  - q: "What does it cost?"
    a: "It's quoted per team size, and the three months is a fixed cost rather than an open-ended engagement, so you know the total before you start. Tell us who you'd send and we'll give you the number, along with what we'd expect to compress first — usually variations."
  - q: "Can it price a job?"
    a: "Pricing stays with your estimator, and we train people to keep it there. AI drafts the written parts of a quote or tender around numbers your estimator owns, which is where the time actually goes."
  - q: "Is it safe to put contract documents into it?"
    a: "With the right tool tier and terms, and that setup is part of the training. Head contracts and commercially sensitive tender material need the business terms rather than a consumer account, and we cover exactly which is which."
  - q: "We're a subcontractor with 20 staff, not a tier-one builder. Is this for us?"
    a: "Smaller businesses usually feel it more, because the person writing the variation is also the person running the job. The unwritten variation problem is a subcontractor problem far more than a head contractor one."
  - q: "What about drawings and models?"
    a: "The program covers the documentation layer — the writing, reading and summarising around the work, which is where the unbilled hours are. Design and modelling tools are a separate purchase and we'll tell you so rather than sell you something we don't teach."
